  "content": {
    "title": "Dreaming of la dolce vita with dual Italian citizenship? Think again",
    "text": "Updated March 14, 2026, 9:04 p.m. ETIf you have an Italian ancestor and thought you might apply for citizenship some day, your task may be getting tougher.Italy's Constitutional Court on March 12 upheld a 2025 law restricting citizenship to persons with an Italian parent or grandparent. Previously, proof of citizenship could go back additional generations to great-grandparents and beyond under the legal provision known as jus sanguinis.Under that \"right of blood\" rule, those who could prove they had an Italian ancestor who was alive after March 17, 1861 – the year Italy became a nation – could seek citizenship and apply for an Italian passport. An Italian passport is much sought after because it provides visa-free entry to more countries than almost any other nationality.That ability to travel across the European Union and to relocate to Italy or other EU countries makes Italian dual citizenship appealing. Nearly 17 million Americans may have Italian ancestry, according to the U.S. Census.Italian Foreign Minister Antonio Tajani – for whom the law, the \"Tajani decree,\" is named after – had criticized abuse of the system, with it being inundated with a surge in people abroad being granted citizenship. \"Being an Italian citizen is a serious thing. It's not a game to get a passport that allows you to go shopping in Miami,\" Tajani said in March 2025, when the new rule was proposed, Reuters reported.The court rejected a challenge arguing the rule, which became law in May 2025, should not retroactively apply to descendants of Italians born before the new law's enaction. In the coming months, courts will consider additional constitutional challenges to the law.“Although this is not the full ruling yet, it already makes clear that the court will not accept constitutional challenges involving citizenship claims beyond the third generation,” Renata Bueno, a Brazilian-born lawyer who lives in Italy and is a former member of the Italian Parliament, told Courthouse News. “The law stands as it was decreed … and later approved by the Italian Parliament.”Italian citizenship rules tightenedThe new law states that Italian citizenship may be given to people whose parent is an Italian citizen born in Italy or whose parent is an Italian citizen born abroad who lived in Italy for at least two consecutive years before the applicant's birth. Those with a grandparent who is an Italian citizen born in Italy are eligible, too.Italy's foreign ministry aimed to address a surge in people abroad being granted citizenship, particularly in South America, where millions of Italians emigrated in the 19th and 20th centuries, often to escape poverty in their home country.Between 2014 and 2024, the number of Italians living abroad rose by 40%, from 4.6 million to 6.4 million. In Argentina alone, Italian citizenship recognitions rose to 30,000 in 2024, from 20,000 in 2023, while Brazil saw a rise to 20,000 from 14,000.Italy has a population of around 59 million, which has been shrinking for the past decade, Reuters reported. The foreign ministry has estimated that under the old rules, 60 million to 80 million people worldwide were eligible for citizenship.Municipal offices were overburdened by companies being paid to help track down documentation needed for citizenship applications, Tajani said previously.\"We are striking down very hard against those who want to make money from the opportunity of becoming an Italian citizen,\" Tajani said, adding that in future, nationality requests would be handled directly in Rome to free up its overburdened consulates around the world.This story has been updated with new information.Contributing: ReutersMike Snider is a national trending news reporter for USA TODAY. You can follow him on Threads, Bluesky, X and email him at mikegsnider & @mikegsnider.bsky.social & @mikesnider & msnider@usatoday.com",
